The lookup now uses a table built once, the first time `GetMesg` is called; afterwards each lookup is a single array access instead of a linear scan.

- **What the table covers:** message numbers below 1024, which are the profile entries that come up most. Each slot stores the index of the first `mesgs` entry with that number, or a "none" marker.
- **Same results as before:**
  - Filling the table forwards and keeping only the first index seen means duplicate numbers resolve to the same entry the old scan returned.
  - A number below 1024 with no entry gives `NULL`, as before.
  - Numbers of 1024 and above, such as manufacturer-specific ranges, still use the original linear scan.
- **Cost:** the table is about 2 KB and is built from the constant `mesgs` data. A function-local static gives thread-safe one-time setup and adds no mutable global state.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    enum { TABLE_SIZE = 1024 };
    struct Table
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx[TABLE_SIZE];
        Table()
        {
            for (int k = 0; k < TABLE_SIZE; k++)
                idx[k] = 0xFFFF;
            for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
            {
                FIT_UINT16 n = mesgs[i].num;
                if (n < TABLE_SIZE && idx[n] == 0xFFFF)
                    idx[n] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
            }
        }
    };
    static const Table table;

    if (num < TABLE_SIZE)
    {
        FIT_UINT16 i = table.idx[num];
        if (i == 0xFFFF)
            return NULL;
        return &mesgs[i];
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
    {
        if (mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }

    return NULL;
}
```